<section image-layout="right" id="9xon2oz6loyrf2d"><img class="accent-image" id="d3Wr1CmmIC" type="image" alt="modern office with glowing screens and people working alongside digital AI interfaces, soft blue ambient light" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/DYS5QSUmPg-69kTqOVEZZ.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:768,&quot;height&quot;:1024}"><div class="body"><h1>AI &amp; the Labor Market: Is AI Disrupting the Future of Work?</h1><p>A structured look at exposure, adoption, and what the evidence actually says.</p></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="3m0iq4gr11w117v"><div class="body"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/VMSOvYY9Zfap-mZNFpbQI.png" source="image.custom"><h1>The Debate Is Real — and the Stakes Are Enormous</h1><smart-layout variant="stats" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item label="$31T"><h4>U.S. Economy</h4><p>Nearly 60% is labor costs — even a small AI shift moves <b>trillions</b> of dollars.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="300M"><h4>Jobs at Risk Globally</h4><p>Roughly 27% of OECD positions could be transformed through AI automation.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item label="71%"><h4>Americans Concerned</h4><p>Worry AI will put too many people out of work permanently — but the reality is more nuanced.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="l0juj5jrx77x8by"><div class="body"><h1>A Job Is a Bundle of Tasks — Not a Single Thing</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/MfIJj4MZa3u3zYdmzsCic.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>AI almost never wipes out whole jobs overnight. It affects <b>tasks first</b> — some get automated, some get supercharged, some create entirely new work.</p><p>A doctor diagnoses, orders tests, interprets results, and coordinates care. A lawyer drafts, researches, argues, and advises. <b>Each task has a different AI exposure profile.</b></p><aside variant="info"><p>The key insight: decompose the job into its tasks before assessing AI's true impact.</p></aside></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="j1oacx5cdqkfe37"><div class="body"><h1>A Three-Layer Framework for Understanding AI's Impact</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/tcJaZfVcfO2bxZF3Nbfe_.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithLabel" cell-size="10" numbered="true"><smart-layout-item><h4>Exposure</h4><p>Which tasks can AI plausibly assist or perform today?</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Adoption</h4><p>How quickly will organizations actually deploy AI?</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Labor Market Response</h4><p>Net effect on employment, wages, and new job creation.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><p>These layers constantly feed back into each other — which is why the debate is so contentious.</p></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="4t5hmafiewbgvqy"><div class="body"><h1>Layer 1: Who Is Most Exposed?</h1><columns col-widths="[35,65]"><div vertical-align="center"><h4>Highest Exposure</h4><p>Text-heavy, information-processing roles: legal research, financial analysis, programming, writing, customer support. High-paying, highly educated jobs score highest because their work is <b>language and symbol dense</b> — a reversal from earlier tech waves that automated routine physical tasks.</p><h4>Lowest Exposure</h4><p>Embodied, on-site, and interpersonal roles: electricians, roofers, nursing assistants, athletes. Physical manipulation and rich social interaction remain largely AI-resistant today.</p></div><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/YUa98dwaWODFnA99ey7mK.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="2xvvt4auvddyx8k"><div class="body"><h1>AI Capabilities Are Racing Ahead of Expectations</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/0EOfYbjRCdmBrLzAIiyt-.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>By 2024, AI surpassed the human baseline on reading comprehension, visual reasoning, PhD-level science questions, and multimodal understanding.</p><p>Researchers now describe a <b>"New Moore's Law"</b>: AI's autonomous task capacity doubles every ~7 months.</p></div></columns><smart-layout variant="minimalTimeline" orientation="vertical" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>2026</h4><p>Completes a 2-hour task autonomously</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>2027</h4><p>Completes a full work day (8 hours)</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>2028</h4><p>Completes a full work week (40 hours)</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>2029</h4><p>Completes a full work month (167 hours)</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ans1hhdxu4mmt7d"><div class="body"><h1>Layer 2: Exposure ≠ Adoption</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/36dqHYHpPqMq2xGj1uh38.png" source="image.custom"><p>A job can be highly exposed in theory but untouched in practice. Legal constraints, union rules, data confidentiality, and workflow inertia all slow deployment.</p><smart-layout variant="solidJoinedBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>54.6%</h4><p>Overall adult Gen AI adoption by August 2025 — up <b>10 points in a single year</b></p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>37.4%</h4><p>Work-specific adoption — still trailing personal use by a significant margin</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="s58zyyawecz8q8t"><div class="body"><h1>The Productivity J-Curve: Why Gains Take Time</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/q-WW-hRgYtpndVD52_C_A.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><p>Like electricity and the PC before it, AI is a <b>general-purpose technology</b>. The PC's productivity gains only appeared in U.S. statistics in the mid-1990s — 15 years after launch — as complementary software, networking, and new work practices matured.</p><aside variant="warning"><p>A 2025 Harvard Business Review study found 40% of knowledge workers spend nearly <b>2 hours per week</b> fixing low-quality AI outputs — a real productivity drag before workflows mature.</p></aside></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="left" id="hmzuy0kr6zfpxt8"><img class="accent-image" id="jalVN96up6" type="image" alt="professional worker at a bright desk collaborating with AI dashboard on screen, confident and productive atmosphere" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/cUvyFqG4y9eMTIggjxjJp.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:768,&quot;height&quot;:1024}"><div class="body"><h1>Response 1: Augmentation — AI Makes Workers More Productive</h1><smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cell-size="20"><smart-layout-item><h4>41% vs. 26%</h4><p>Daily AI users rating their productivity "Very Good" compared to non-users — plus measurable gains in focus, stress management, and job satisfaction.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>The Radiology Lesson</h4><p>Geoff Hinton predicted radiologists replaced within 5 years (2016). AI did outperform on benchmarks — but the field kept growing. Exposure and replacement are not the same thing.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="1gx83z0qbnntz0h"><div class="body"><h1>Response 2: Displacement — Real, But Concentrated</h1><columns col-widths="[35,65]"><div vertical-align="center"><p>Clerical and back-office roles face the highest displacement risk: routine document processing, booking, transcription, and standardized communications are repeatedly flagged as highly automatable.</p><blockquote><p>"Look for jobs that are repetitive, independent, closed, short, forgiving, and digital — those are the ones most at risk on shorter timescales." — Andrej Karpathy</p></blockquote></div><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/bbGVZndFwjHmGLZpma0Bu.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="right" id="72bqwrqwd1k106x"><img class="accent-image" id="4PtI1lhB4S" type="image" alt="workers transitioning to new digital roles in a bright modern workspace, collaborative and optimistic tone" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/p7r4it3una5hm8z/generated-images/ePsJQj5-2siPqjhmh99yE.png" source="image.ai-generated" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:768,&quot;height&quot;:1024}"><div class="body"><h1>Response 3: Reinstatement — AI Creates New Roles Too</h1><smart-layout variant="arrowBullets" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/arrow-right.svg" source="image.icon"></icon><div><h4>History Rhymes</h4><p>The PC era created software engineers, IT support, and digital marketers that didn't exist before. Technology revolutions rarely destroy jobs in aggregate — they transform them.</p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/arrow-right.svg" source="image.icon"></icon><div><h4>New AI-Era Occupations</h4><p>AI engineers, prompt specialists, AI trainers, and trust &amp; safety roles are already emerging. Productivity gains lower costs, raise demand, and expand existing occupations.</p></div></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><icon class="accessory-image" src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/arrow-right.svg" source="image.icon"></icon><div><h4>The Real Challenge</h4><p>The <b>pace and distribution</b> of change — who adapts fastest and who benefits most — will determine whether this transition is broadly shared or deeply unequal.</p></div></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="l7466ybzv0pecwp"><div class="body"><h1>What to Watch: Three Questions That Will Determine the Outcome</h1><columns col-widths="[65,35]"><div vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/9yfLWL3zkz5f1WStGFzWZ.png" source="image.custom" align="center"></div><div vertical-align="center"><smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine" cell-size="10"><smart-layout-item><h4>Will adoption accelerate or plateau?</h4><p>If AI capability doubles every 4–7 months, the window for workers and institutions to adapt narrows dramatically.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>Who captures the productivity gains?</h4><p>Higher-wage, higher-educated workers are most exposed — and most positioned to benefit. Lower-wage workers face less disruption but fewer gains.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>How will policy respond?</h4><p>Retraining, labor protections, and safety nets will shape whether AI's abundance is broadly shared or concentrated at the top.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout></div></columns></div></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="ghnwu8jgzlgm0jz"><div class="body"><h1>Sources</h1><ul><li><a href="#">AI and labor market PDF</a></li></ul></div></section>